how did brands gain such control in our  lives and how will brands function in a  rapidly changing world this three-part  series entitled the 22nd century brand  is brought to you by design Singapore  the national agency for design enabling  Singapore to use design for economic  growth and to improve lives  in these discussions we will be looking  at the origin of brands how brands  function in our contemporary cultural  landscape and the future of brands where  it's all headed my name is hunter Tara  I'm the founder and CEO of an  international brand design consultancy  called syndicate X over the past 25  years I've had the opportunity to work  with some of the world's leading  organizations across culture commerce  and technology and I've helped them  develop clear brand stories and I've  helped them articulate their value  proposition I've worked with  multinational corporations such as  Netflix Nike GE Unilever Lululemon OD  and Asics to some of the world's leading  museums and cultural institutions  including the Victoria and Albert Museum  and the John F Kennedy Center for the  Performing Arts through these  collaborative partnerships I've had the  opportunity to see how brands really  work and to witness the power they have  in our collective consciousness this  series is intended to share some of the  insights and secrets I've been able to  gather over the course of my career our  first discussion is on the origin of  brands and in particular we will explore  the peculiar set of dynamics that have  made brands such a ubiquitous part of  contemporary life and how our  affiliation with brands has displaced  our fundamental connections to nature  and to one another  first we're going to start with a little  test for those of you watching at home  I'm wondering how many of you can name  these six types of trees  no don't feel bad I've done this test  over the years many times with my  students and at conferences and in that  time only one guy got all six and he was  a landscape architect now let's try  another test can you name these six  brands I thought so but this test is  meant to illustrate is how our  understanding of and our connection to  brands has fundamentally displaced  humankind's basic relationship to our  natural surroundings if you think about  it five hundred years ago if you didn't  know which trees these were you could be  poisoned or worse and today we can't  even identify what surrounds us what we  do know is in our contemporary age  brands are everywhere  let's also start with some basic  terminology  [Music]  we hear a lot about brands and branding  in business and culture and in fact  almost everything today is considered a  brand consumer products content  influencers even cities such as  Singapore considered brands that have  standards and guidelines that govern  their image and communications but let's  keep it simple when I talk about brand  what I'm really talking about is the  image and reputation of a company or  organization and when I talk about  branding that is how an organization or  company uses design and language to  maintain that image and reputation  simple right well yes and no and that's  what we're going to explore more deeply  in this series  [Music]  how did brands gain such control in our  lives and how old brands function in a  rapidly changing world with  technological innovation a world made  even more uncertain by the Cova 19  pandemic before I went into a career in  design I actually trained to be a  historian and I still think about things  in historical terms how to understand  our current and future state through the  lens of the past and what the lessons of  the past can teach us about where we're  going this is a diagram I drew a few  years ago for a project in China I think  it's still relevant and explaining the  evolution of brands over the past 200  years we'll get into the 21st and 22nd  century in later episodes but today  we're gonna go back in time to  understand where we came from  historically brands have been thought of  as static entities trademarks that  represent a product or service and  denote a certain quality and consistency  when looking through the historical lens  of brand we can begin to see unified  systems as far back as the Roman Empire  this image is an excerpt from my  graduate thesis done as part of the  Harvard project on the city under the  direction of the Dutch architect REM  koolhaas although historians have  traditionally presented Rome as the  basis of Western civilization through  legal codes cultural expression  infrastructure and trade in fact Rome  might be thought of as the first truly  global brand as Rome expanded across  most of Europe and into the Middle East  Africa and parts of Asia they used a  consistent visual language in their  buildings uniforms and currency even in  the layout of their cities  I like to say that Rome was the original  Starbucks well every Starbucks store has  slight variations in its design and  layout when you're in a Starbucks  anywhere in the world it's immediately  recognizable as a brand by the  Renaissance in Europe and with the  resurgence of global commerce we begin  to see the consistent usage of brand  imagery for example the artois brewery  in Leuven Belgium has been using the  same logo since 1366 it only added the  now iconic star element in the 1920s  with the advent of the Industrial Age  and the rising notion of intellectual  property brands worried about Impostors  what today we call knock-offs  as every student of branding knows a  sale was the first registered trademark  in the UK in 1877  it's my belief that an alternative  history of the 20th century can be told  through the impact of the brands that  defined its direction from the iconic  script logo of the Ford Motor Company  which represented mass industrialization  in corporate efficiency the  professionalization of management and  corporate efficiency brought to a global  scale it opened up attainable personal  mobility to millions for the first time  but also it brought with it the negative  effects of our reliance on fossil fuel  and our impact on the natural  environment to the peace symbol shown  here the now infamous 1968 Democratic  National Convention in Chicago which  came to symbolize the global  counterculture movement of the 1960s and  is still used to represent a series of  values and principles related to  environmentalism social justice equal  rights and non-violence to Steve Jobs  Apple logo a logo that is synonymous  with technological innovation and  increasingly represents the power of  great design to make an impact and  improve our lives  [Music]  as my colleague Bruce malwan said  without design Apple is a reasonably  good technology company and with design  is the most valuable corporation in the  history of the planet so what do all of  these logos have in common  they've become representative for the  large-scale social economic cultural  technological changes that have  characterized the century during what I  would call the high period of brand  design the role the designer itself as  an inspired author or a tour of singular  iconic memorable imagery for brand was  established in the cultural imagination  here we see Paul Rand in his studio  coincidentally the designer of Steve  Jobs next load owns the company he  developed during his period of exile  from Apple in the 1990s with some of his  iconic design work pinned up in the  background on the walls of the studio  it's the perfect example an image of the  20th century designer and one that still  exists in the cultural imagination today  [Music]  in 1982 everything changed through a  series of cultural events and business  innovations design has now become  digital and it's now possible for brands  to express themselves in new and  innovative ways and the tools and  techniques of brand expression have now  become accessible to common people in  1982 Adobe was founded Adobe is the  company that is most closely associated  with creating the tools of creative  expression and 1982 Blade Runner was  released changing the way we think about  media and advertisement especially in  cities and public spaces  what's amazing looking at these images  today Blade Runner predicted the  contemporary bourbon condition in global  cities in the United States  MTV reached 1 million households for the  first time in 1982 as a child of the  1980s I can attest to the lasting impact  it made on visual culture it changed  everything that had come before motion  graphics set design costumes hair and  makeup  [Music]  television advertising media would never  be the same and it changed how we think  about brands and of course the computer  was named Time magazine's machine of the  year up to that point Man of the Year  digital technology was now part of  everyday life an increasingly linked to  how we think about the expression of  brands by 1984 when Apple released the  first Macintosh computer and the  now-infamous  corresponding 1984 Super Bowl ad that  year design had become democratized for  the first time putting the tools and  techniques of brand design in the hands  of common people  [Music]  [Music]
 

